                <pb facs="0959" n="857" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg857"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">818.00/867: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at San José</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Chase</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >San José</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-09-01T15:00:00-05:36"><hi rendition="#italic">September 1,
                                1919, 3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received
                            September 3, 12.50 p.m.</hi>]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Your August 30, 1 p.m. I saw Quiros this morning and advised him of that
                    instruction. He asked that publicity be delayed 72 hours and said that he was
                    afraid of manifestations and possible bloodshed. He said that he was not sure
                    the military in control of some barracks. Barquero has made the same request, so
                    I am waiting. In view of this danger a man of war at each port would prevent <pb
                        facs="0960" n="858" xml:id="pg858"/> any attempt by Tinoco adherents to
                    cause trouble. It would be of great benefit to both men and I believe it would
                    be welcome to both of them.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Chase</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
